<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Butrus al-Bustani</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1819">1819</a>–<a href="/years/1883">1883</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Boutros al-Boustani</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Butrus al-Bustani was a writer and scholar from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lebanon">Mount Lebanon</a>, a major figure of the Nahda, the Arab renaissance. Born a Maronite, he became a Protestant while working for American missionaries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a> and helped translate the Bible into Arabic. After the 1860 civil war his Nafir Suriyya, the Clarion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, appealed for unity in a Syrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a>, and in 1863 he founded the secular National School in Beirut, open to all religions. He compiled the dictionary Muhit al-Muhit, founded the journal al-Jinan in 1870 and in 1876 began the encyclopedia Da'irat al-Ma'arif.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/butrus-al-bustani.jpg" alt="Butrus al-Bustani" width="320" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al_Mu'allim_Butrus_al-Bustani.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
